At the respective interfaces, the ETL and HTL extract the free electrons and holes provided that the conduction band (CB) and valence band (VB) of the perovskite layer align with the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) or the CB of the ETL, and the highest unoccupied molecular orbital (HOMO) or the VB of the HTL, respectively (Figure 3b).[50]
The ETL and HTL in turn should form Ohmic contacts with the electrodes (highly conductive contacts) which is decided by the work function of the electrodes.[59] As such, the interfaces play an important role
in the function of a PSC and can contribute to losses through non-radiative recombination and charge transport barriers as a result of energy mismatch[59] and thus should be properly engineered.[63] Proper selection of electrodes and interface materials (interlayers) for Pb-Sn PSCs will be discussed next in Section 2, and the perovskite absorber layer will be further discussed in Section 3.
## 2.2. Device Architectures and Interlayer Materials for Pb-Sn Mixed PSCs
## 2.2.1. Device Architectures
A PSC device can be either of the "standard/ regular" (n-i-p) architecture; with the sequence of transparent cathode/ETL/perovskite/HTL/metal anode, or of the "inverted"
(p-i-n) architecture; with transparent anode/HTL/perovskite/ETL/ metal cathode sequence.[63] Although in Pb-only PSCs with record efficiencies, the standard architecture is commonly reported (Figure 4a),[48] the inverted configuration is most widely used in Sn-based or Pb-Sn mixed PSCs[15,64] (Figure 4b), which allows better suitability for low-temperature device fabrication. The charge transport layers used in the inverted configuration are mostly inspired by OPVs (such as polymers and fullerene derivatives) suited for low-temperature large area PVs.[15,64] Thus, Pb-Sn PSCs, this limits the use of doped HTL layers such as Spiro-OMeTAD which is commonly used in Pb-only standard device,[65] alleviating any dopant induced in the highly reactive Sn-incorporated perovskites.
## 2.2.2. Electrode Materials
The common electrode materials and charge transport materials for PSCs are given in Figure 5. While it is necessary that one of the electrodes has high light transmission (front contact), depending on the application, the back contact electrode can also be made semi/transparent.[66,67] However, in single-junction Pb-Sn PSCs, the back contacts are still made of highly reflective metals which also serves the function of reflecting the light back toward the perovskite layer. Typical Pb-only PSCs employ thin films \(({\sim}100{-}150\ \mathrm{nm})\) of Au or Ag,[63] and Pb-Sn mixed PSCs mostly use \(\mathrm{Ag}^{[11,11,14]}\) and in some cases \(\mathrm{Al}^{[2,20,68]}\) electrodes, owing to their high reflectivity and suitable work functions (Ag \({\sim}4.3\ \mathrm{eV}\) and Al \({\sim}4.2\ \mathrm{eV}\) ) that align well with fullerene-derived ETLs. In one study on all-inorganic Pb-Sn mixed PSCs by Liang et al,[22] a carbon electrode was employed to replace the metal back contact as well as the HTL in a standard device architecture. The authors achieved a very high \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}\) of \(1.26~\mathrm{V}\) with a \(\mathrm{C_{10}P b_{0.9}S n_{0.1}I B r_{2}}\) absorber, while avoiding unstable organic components for HTL and expensive noble metal electrodes. Use of Cu electrodes has also been reported by the Tan group,[34,45] resulting in \(21.1\%\) and \(21.7\%\) efficient \(\mathrm{MA}_{0.3}\mathrm{FA}_{0.7}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) PSC; however, these high efficiencies were attributed by the authors to other modifications of the cell and not the choice of electrode metal alone. The transparent electrode in a PSC is typically a conductive oxide layer such as ITO (indium-doped tin oxide) or FTO (fluorine-doped tin oxide) given their high light transmission, conductivity, and reproducibility[59,66] (apart from PSCs, ITO is employed in OPVs, microcrystalline Si cells and CIGS, and FTO in aSi cells, DSSCs (dye-sensitized solar cells) and CdTe PVs).[69] The vast majority of Pb-Sn-mixed and other low-temperature processed inverted PSCs employ ITO,[11,16,17,59,64] although a limited number of studies also employed FTO electrodes.[30,36] ITO electrodes are attractive candidates for flexible PSCs (ITO-coated PET (polyethylene terephthalate) and PEN (polyethylene naphthalate substrates), although flexible Pb-Sn mixed perovskites are not yet reported widely. FTO needs high-processing temperatures, limiting its suitability to be deposited on plastic substrates. However, in some cases indium diffusion into the adjacent layers was reported with ITO,[70] whereas FTO has more stability (against high temperatures and physical abrasion), thus more suited for Pb-only standard devices which requires high-processing temperatures of \({\sim}400{-}500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for ETL deposition (e.g., \(\mathrm{ITO}_{2}\)).
and PEN (polyethylene naphthalate substrates), although flexible Pb-Sn mixed perovskites are not yet reported widely. FTO needs highprocessing temperatures, limiting its suitability to be deposited on plastic substrates. However, in some cases indium diffusion into the adjacent layers was reported with ITO,[70] whereas FTO has more stability (against high temperatures and physical abrasion), thus more suited for Pb-only standard devices which requires high-processing temperatures of \({\sim}400{-}500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for ETL deposition (e.g., \(\mathrm{ITO}_{2}\) ).
Contrary to the popular use, Kapil et al. replaced ITO with FTO in inverted PSCs with a \((\mathrm{Cs_{x}FA_{1-x}SnI_{3}})_{0.5}(\mathrm{MAPbI_{3}})_{0.5}\) absorber to find higher external quantum efficiency (EQE) reaching \(80\%\) at \(900\ \mathrm{nm}\).[71] The authors point out that the widespread assumption that high surface roughness of FTO may lead to short circuits in the device, may have limited the use of FTO in Pb-Sn mixed PSCs, and highlight the benefit of FTO in allowing higher light transmission in the NIR region. Over \(800\ \mathrm{nm}\), Pb-Sn PSCs in this work exhibited higher \(\mathrm{J_{SC}}\) (improving overall from \(29.1\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) to \(31.58\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\)) increasing the PCE from \(16.7\%\) to \(17.7\%\).[71]
## 2.2.3. Charge Selective Layers
Electron Transport Layers: Solution-processable ETL materials can be either inorganic (mainly transition metal oxides), or organic materials (fullerenes and derivatives),[72] where the latter is used in most of the Pb-Sn mixed PSCs (Figure 5). A high-electron mobility and proper energy-level alignment with the perovskite CB are vital in selecting a suitable ETL.[73]
Inorganic ETLs: Transition metal oxides were initially used in DSSC technology and widely seen now in standard PSC architecture.[63] In high-performing Pb-PSCs, the first and most common inorganic ETL is \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) owing to its high-electron collection efficiency. In Pb-Sn PSCs however, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) can only be seen in a few very early reports[10] and in all-inorganic Pb-Sn PSCs.[22] Its need for very high temperatures \((\sim\!400\) \(-\ 500\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) in fabrication,[74] instability under UV exposure, and low-electron mobility hinders widespread commercial applications.[73,75] Therefore, other transition metal oxides such as \(\mathrm{ZnO}^{[76]}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}^{[77]}\) are employed (cast from nanoparticle solutions) in Pb-PSCs as alternatives to \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), of which the former was also adopted for Pb-Sn mixed PSCs.[7,47,78] \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) can be used to improve the band alignment between the metal electrode and fullerene-based ETLs or as an optical spacer, is solution processable at room temperature, and does not need further annealing.[47] Despite the attractive qualities of \(\mathrm{ZnO}\), the long-term chemical instability hinders its application.[79] In contrast, \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), although not widely explored in Pb-Sn PSCs, has higher stability in ambient conditions and needs relatively low fabrication temperatures of \(\sim\!180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).[79] \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-EDTA (ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid) complexes were also reported in Pb-only devices as means of reducing the hysteresis in devices with this ETL,[80] which resulted in record efficiencies of \(21.5\%\) for a \(\mathrm{FA}_{0.95}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) containing PSC.
Organic ETLs: Organic ETLs were inspired by OPVs and are widely used in Pb-Sn mixed PSCs, of which the most common are fullerenes such as \(\mathrm{C}_{60}^{[13,15,68,81]}\) and bis-\(\mathrm{C}_{60}^{[9,25,27,28]}\) and their derivatives such as \(\mathrm{PC}_{60}\mathrm{BM}\) ([6,6]-phenyl-C 61-butyric acid methyl ester),\(\mathrm{PC}_{70}\mathrm{BM}\)
([6,6]-phenyl-C 7,1-butyric acid methyl ester),[7,47] and ICBA (indene\(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) bisadduct).[5,20,22] Additionally, some have reported other organic molecules such as Phen-NaDPO.[82] A proper match between the perovskite CB and the LUMO of the organic ETL has to be ensured for efficient electron extraction. A comparative study on 6 different fullerene derivatives as ETL was reported by Lin et al., employing Pb-Sn PSCs with a \(\mathrm{MAPb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) perovskite absorber (Figure 6).[19] The authors reported a strong dependence of device \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}\) on the LUMO level of the fullerene derivative used. From the tested ETLs, the novel fullerene derivative BPB-M showed the best results with a PCE of \(10.24\%\) \((\mathrm{J_{SC}}~\,22.8~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}\) , \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}~\,0.69~\mathrm{V}\) and FF \(65\%\) ) followed by PCBM and \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) . These have LUMO levels of \(-3.74,\ -3.68,\) and \(-3.78\,\mathrm{~eV}\) respectively (relative to vacuum level), lower than the CB of \(\mathrm{MAPb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) perovskite \((-3.67\,\mathrm{~eV}\) relative to vacuum level) as depicted in Figure 6b.
In Pb-Sn mixed PSCs, both \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) and its derivatives are considered good ETL candidates with low-temperature and large area processability, excellent band alignment, and sufficient electrical conductivities, although their electron mobilities are considerably inferior to inorganic materials.[63,72] Pb-Sn mixed PSCs often use two or more organic ETLs are used together, through sequential deposition of \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) or bis- \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) with PCBM or ICBA.[9,11,26,28] In some cases, \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) was employed as a surface passivation layer,[36] which reportedly reduced the pinholes on perovskite film, passivated the perovskite-ETL (PCBM) interface, and protected the perovskite from exposure to oxygen and moisture. Employing several ETLs together also allows favorable band alignment in PSCs, depending on the perovskite CB, and improves efficiency of electron extraction. In other reports, \(\mathrm{PC}_{60}\mathrm{BM}\) surface was coated with inorganic materials such as \(_{\mathrm{ZnO}}\) (nanoparticles),[78] metal-organic materials such as Zr-Acac,[21] or other organic materials such as NaDPO,[82] to compose the ETL.
Furthermore, in many Pb-Sn mixed PSCs, a thin \((\sim\!5\!-\!7\ \mathrm{nm})\) film of bathocuproine (BCP) is typically employed as a buffer layer at the ETL/electrode interface.[15,30,36,68,83] BCP is an organic small molecule material, used as an "interlayer" for improvement of interfaces which critically affect the charge transport and recombination dynamics in PSCs and OPVs.[83] It is reported that BCP aids in improving the metal electrode and organic ETL contact through reducing charge accumulation at this interface and reducing recombination.[83,84] The vast majority of Pb-Sn mixed PSCs employ PCBM/BCP/Ag or \(\mathrm{C_{60}/B C P/A g}\) to comprise the device cathode. Inorganic interface layers such as \(\mathrm{LiF}^{[19]}\) and Al-doped \(\mathrm{ZnO}^{[7,47]}\) have also been reported in some cases for Pb-Sn mixed perovskites.
## 2.3. Hole Transport Layers
The solution-processable HTLs can also be either inorganic or organic materials (Figure 5), with the latter being most regular for Pb-Sn mixed PSCs. In standard Pb-based PSCs, the most popular HTL is the organic small molecule Spiro-OMeTAD \((2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}\) -tertrakis(N,N-pdimethoxyphenylamino)-9,9′-spirobifluorene) system,[85] which was initially explored for Pb-Sn PSCs in very early reports (standard device architecture).[10] To enable better conductivity, Spiro-OMeTAD needs to be partially oxidized by a combination of dopants (LiTFSi (lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide), TBP (tributyl phosphate), and FK209 (tris(2-(1H-pyrazol-1-yl)-4-tert-butylpyridine) cobalt(III)),[64,86] which can give rise to degradation of the perovskites through reacting with the \(\Gamma\) ions, despite the high performance of the HTL.[87] This effect is much severe in Pb-Sn mixed perovskites and other Sn-incorporated perovskites, due to their inherent high reactivity.[65] Therefore, it is best that Pb-Sn mixed PSCs (as well as Sn-only)
to employ HTLs that do not necessitate doping,[65] and as such, PEDOT:PSS (poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene):poly(styrenesulfonate)) is commonly used in this field.[28,45,88]
PEDOT:PSS is a conductive polymer blend with a work function of \({\sim}5.1{-}5.2\ \mathrm{eV}\), initially used in OPVs. The suitable work function alignment with perovskite absorbers, water solubility enabling easy ambient processing without toxic solvents, and large area processability has made this material attractive in inverted PSCs. Despite the inherent acidity of PEDOT:PSS which reportedly induce corrosion of the ITO electrode,[89] it is still the preferred HTL in many Sn-incorporated \(\mathrm{PSCs}^{[65]}\) since it is sufficiently conductive to avoid dopants. However, PEDOT:PSS has also been infamous for its role in giving rise to poor morphology of perovskite layers (high defect density from pinholes and open grain boundaries), and inferior interface band alignment resulting in poor electron blocking capability.[8,9,78,81] Moreover, in Sn-containing perovskites, any residual water in the PEDOT:PSS layer can accelerate \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation. Thus, other organic polymers such as \(\mathrm{PCP-Na}^{[81]}\) and PTAA,[78] have been used as HTL in \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed PSCs, as well as modification of the PEDOT:PSS layer itself. In these occasions, an increase in film quality of the perovskite absorber among other improvements was reported, in contrast to PEDOT:PSS, leading to device efficiency
improvements.[78,81] In a study by Yao et al,[88] (Figure 7a-c) PSC efficiency improvements were achieved by simply using a highly diluted PEDOT:PSS solution (1:8 in IPA) as HTL for \(\mathrm{MA}_{0.4}\mathrm{FA}_{0.6}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.4}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.6}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) cells. The PCE improved from \(17.79\%\) (undiluted HTL) to \(19.50\%\) (diluted HTL, with a further optimized device of \(19.58\%\) ) attributed to a more favorable energy-level alignment of the diluted HTL (work function \(-4.85\ \mathrm{eV}\)) with the perovskite VB level \((-4.98\ \mathrm{eV})\), than the undiluted PEDOT:PSS (work function \(-5.04\ \mathrm{eV}\)). In the recent work by Zhang et al,[90] PEDOT:PSS was modified with hydroquinone (HQ) to yield a \(14.65\%\) efficiency (from \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\,85\%\) of reference) for \(\mathrm{MA}_{0.7}\mathrm{FA}_{0.3}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.7}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.3}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) PSCs. Here, the HQ treatment was carried out in keeping with other reports that used p-dopants or high boiling point solvents to reduce sheet resistance of PEDOT:PSS and improve conductivity of the HTL.[90] The authors report a lower interface barrier between the HTL and the perovskite film, lower leakage current, and improved grain sizes of \({\sim}1~\upmu\mathrm{m}\) with HQ-modified PEDOT:PSS, with the maximum improvements achieved at \(0.5\ \mathrm{wt\%}\) HQ (in IPA) spin-coated on top of PEDOT:PSS.
In a study using an alternate organic HTL, PCP-Na, Shao et al[81] achieved a more compact, pinhole free film morphology for a \(\mathrm{FAPb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) perovskite fabricated on the new HTL over a large area compared to the reference film on PEDOT:PSS, which effectively elimi
nated trap states and shunts reducing interfacial and bulk recombination.[81] The champion PCE was increased from \(11.6\%\) (PEDOT) to \(16.27\%\) (PCP-Na) with significant improvements of all the device parameters (VOC, JSC, and FF from \(0.61\ \mathrm{V}\), \(27.80\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) and \(68\%\) (PEDOT) to \(0.78\ \mathrm{V}\), \(28.51\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) and \(73\%\) (PCP-Na)) (Figure 7d,e), owing to the improved film quality as well as a more favorable energy alignment at the anode interface due to higher work function of PCP-Na. Furthermore, PEDOT:PSS morphology is reportedly of an isotropic nature while PCP-Na assumed a more crystalline structure with its backbone parallel to the ITO/substrate surface which allowed improved charge extraction.
In a study employing PTAA as the HTL, Wang et al achieved \(13.6\%\) PCE (compared to \(9.6\%\) for PEDOT:PSS reference) for a PSC containing \(\mathrm{FA}_{0.7}\mathrm{MA}_{0.3}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.7}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.3}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) double cation perovskite.[78] Here, the improvements were attributed to large \((\sim1\ \upmu\mathrm{m})\) perovskite grain growth on PTAA compared to the small grains on PEDOT:PSS with vertically aligned grain boundaries (Figure 7f). The more hydrophobic nature of PTAA reduces the surface tension dragging force and the number of nucleation sites available for the perovskite, resulting in high grain boundary mobility and larger grains. Perovskite layer grown on PTAA also resulted in a lower trap density, higher crystallinity, and higher
hole mobility. In another study using PTAA as HTL, Chang et al[91] tested the effect of cross-linking and p-doping of PTAA for n-1-p devices with a \(\mathrm{MAPb}_{0.75}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.25}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) absorber and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{PCBM}\) ETLs. Here, TPEMN3 was used as the cross-linking agent and \(\mathrm{Mo}(\mathrm{tfdCOCF}_{3})_{3}\) as the pdopant. Four types of HTLs (untreated PTAA, cross-linked PTAA, doped cross-linked PTAA, and Spiro-OMeTAD) were tested with resulting champion PCEs of \(12.12\%\) , \(12.57\%\) , \(15.55\%\) , and \(13.31\%\) , respectively, indicating the highest performance for doped cross-linked PTAA. Cross-linking under heating and light exposure was reported to form a 3D polymer network that increases the layer's solvent resistance, maintaining the structural integrity of the film upon deposition of other layers of the PSC. The cross-linked PTAA also showed a higher light transmittance and no adverse impacts on the charge transport mechanisms of the perovskite films. Furthermore, it reportedly improved the device stability significantly, especially compared to the PSCs with Spiro-OMeTAD HTL. The pdopant improved the conductivity of HTL by orders of magnitude, as well as shifting the HTL work function upwards, allowing better hole transport from the absorber to the HTL.
In parallel to polymeric HTLs, inorganic materials have also been explored as HTLs in Pb-Sn mixed perovskites to improve the stability and reproducibility of the resulting devices and to obtain high conductivity of the transport layer. In Pb-based perovskites, some of the inorganic HTLs used include \(\mathrm{NiO_{x}}\) ,[92] Cu,[93] and CuSCN,[94] resulting in comparable efficiencies to Spiro-OMeTAD with better stability and reproducibility. Among those, \(\mathrm{NiO_{x}}\) was employed in some reports for Pb-Sn mixed perovskites.[2,8,95] Zhao et al have employed a \(\mathrm{NiO_{x}}/\mathrm{PEDOT:PSS}\) bilayer as the HTL in their work with \(\mathrm{MAPb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) PSCs,[2] which resulted in large micron-sized grains. Here, the \(\mathrm{NiO_{x}}\) layer was casted on top of the PEDOT:PSS film to provide the base for the perovskite growth. The perovskite exhibited a low structural disorder and achieved full surface coverage. In other work, Chi et al reported the complete substitution of PEDOT:PSS with \(\mathrm{NiO_{x}}\) as HTL in \(\mathrm{FAPb}_{0.75}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.25}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) containing PSCs,[8] on the premise of \(\mathrm{NiO_{x}}\) 's ability to trigger perovskite crystallization and enhance hole extraction. The authors achieved compact, pinhole-free perovskite layers with larger crystals \((\sim\!1\ \upmu\mathrm{m})\) on \(\mathrm{NiO_{x}}\) HTL compared to smaller crystal sizes and pinhole-rich perovskite growth on PEDOT:PSS (Figure 8a-d). The improvements were attributed to the inferior surface wetting properties of \(\mathrm{NiO_{x}}\) compared to the highly hydrophilic nature of PEDOT:PSS. A more hydrophobic HTL surface is known to give rise to less nucleation sites resulting in larger grain growth.[8,78] The champion PCE of the devices with \(\mathrm{NiO_{x}}\) was reported as \(17.25\%\) under reverse scan, compared to \(14.24\%\) for devices with PEDOT:PSS. Furthermore, devices exhibited low hysteresis and high stability ( \(91\%\) of initial PCE maintained at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(20~\mathrm{h}\) , and \(92\%\) retained after 46 days at room temperature). Here, the \(\mathrm{NiO_{x}}\) film needed high-temperature annealing \((300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\ 1\ \mathrm{h})\) to fabricate the HTL. However, a room temperature processed \(\mathrm{NiO_{x}}\) nanocrystal ink \(\left(\mathrm{L-NiO}_{\mathrm{x}}\right)\) as HTL was reported in a very recent study by Chen et al (Figure 8e-i), resulting in highly efficient (champion PCE \(18.77\%\) and average \(18.18\,\pm\,0.30)\) \(\mathrm{MA}_{0.4}\mathrm{FA}_{0.6}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.4}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.6}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) PSCs with negligible hysteresis and high stability ( \(96\%\) retention of original PCE after 50 days under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) ).[96] The authors compared these results against conventional high-temperature processed \(\mathrm{NiO_{x}}\) \(\left(\mathrm{H-NiO_{x}}\right)\) annealed at \(250\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and reference PEDOT:PSS (annealed at \(175\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) containing devices which exhibited champion efficiencies of \(15.85\%\) and \(17.03\%\) , respectively. Here, \(\mathrm{L-NiO_{x}}\) HTL showed a deeper VB than the \(\mathrm{H-NiO_{x}}\) (Figure \(8\mathrm{g}\) ), allowing better hole extraction from the perovskite layer, as well as slightly higher grain sizes and a lower trap density that decreased charge carrier recombination of the devices. The high quality and lower interface defect density of \(\mathrm{L-NiO_{x}}\) were also ascribed to
suppressing the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation of the films, which translated to device stability. However, although no annealing of \(\mathrm{L-NiO_{x}}\) was conducted in the fabrication process, the precursor nanocrystal ink preparation needed a \(270~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) calcination step, whereas \(\mathrm{H-NiO_{x}}\) precursor solution only needed \(50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .
## 3. Pb-Sn Perovskite Absorber
## 3.1. Crystal Structure
"Perovskites" (named after the Russian scientist Lev Perovski) have a general formula of \(\mathrm{ABX}_{3}\) , and a simple cubic unit cell comprising of a cations A and B \((\mathrm{A}>\mathrm{B})\) and anions X as depicted in Figure 9a.[96] The type of perovskites used in PSCs is "Metal Halide Perovskites" where X is a halide and B is a metal cation,[97,98] and have photoactive and semiconducting properties, with suitable bandgaps \((\sim\!1\!-\!3\mathrm{~eV})\) suitable for photovoltaics and optoelectronic devices.[59,98] Here, as used in Pb-Sn perovskites, the A-site is a monovalent cation, either inorganic (such as \({\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}\) ) or organic (such as \({\mathrm{CH}}_{3}{\mathrm{NH}}_{3}{}^{+}\) (methyl ammonium or \(\mathrm{MA}^{+}\) ), CH \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)_{2}{}^{+}\) (formamidinium or \(\mathrm{FA^{+}}\) ), while X is commonly \(\Gamma^{-}\) (although occasionally \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) and \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) are incorporated).[97,98] The B-site cation in this case is a divalent group 14 metal cations \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) .[98] The \(\mathrm{ABX}_{3}\) structure is a combination of a cubic closely packed lattice of \(\mathrm{AX}_{3}\) (Figure 9a) and \(\mathrm{BX}_{6}\) octahedra stabilizing the B cation. The perovskite lattice has to achieve both charge neutrality and balance among the ionic radii, where \(\mathrm{BX}_{6}\) stability is decided by the "octahedral factor \((\upmu)\) " (Equation 1). This takes ratio between the ionic radii \(\mathrm{r_{B}}\) and \(\mathrm{r_{X}}\) into account, which should be between 0.442 and 0.895 for a stable perovskite structure.[98]
\[
\mathrm{Octahedralfactor}(\mu)=\frac{\mathrm{r_B}}{\mathrm{r_x}}
\]
Another parameter, "Goldschmidt tolerance factor (t)", factors in A-site cation radius \(\mathrm{r_{A}}\) Equation 2, and should be between 0.8 and 1.0.[98]
\[
\mathrm{Goldschmidt~tolerance~factor~(t)=}\frac{\left(\mathrm{r}_A+\mathrm{r}_X\right)}{\sqrt{2}\left(\mathrm{r}_B+\mathrm{r}_X\right)}.
\]
The \(\mathrm{ABX}_{3}\) crystal takes an almost ideal cubic structure when \(0.9<\mathrm{t}\) \(\leq\ 1\) \((\mathrm{t}=1\) ideal) and takes a tetragonal or orthorhombic structure under \(0.8<\mathrm{t}<0.9\) . Generally, \(\mathrm{t}>1\) results when A-cation is too large and \(\mathrm{t}<0.8\) when A is too small, both not allowing a structurally stable metal halide perovskite.
distance through the size of the A-cation radius.[101] Thus, a large A-cation such as \(\mathrm{FA^{+}}\) will occupy more space in the perovskite lattice and decrease the B-X distance, thereby indirectly decreasing the bandgap (Figure 9c,d).[101] Similarly, a small cation such as \(\mathrm{MA^{+}}\) will increase the bandgap.
## 3.2. Compositional Engineering of Pb-Sn Mixed Perovskites
As noted in the previous section, Pb-Sn mixed PSCs have been investigated in a wide variety of \(\mathrm{ABX}_{3}\) formulae. The reported work on
various Pb:Sn ratio and different A-cations and X anions are given in Table 1 and discussed in the following sections.
## 3.2.1. A-site Engineering
In metal halide perovskites for PSCs, using a combination of different cations such as \(\mathrm{FA^{+}}\) and \(\mathrm{MA^{+}}\) in the A-site is preferred over single cation systems, due to the complementary qualities brought together to the absorber layer (such as higher thermal stability from \(\mathrm{FA^{+}}\) and higher structural stability from \(\mathrm{MA^{+}}\)).[102] These multication systems were
initially synthesized for Pb-only perovskites and have since been adopted for Pb-Sn mixed PSCs. Thus, they can be best understood by briefly reviewing Pb-only systems first. For instance, higher structural stability in \(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}\) was reported when the formula was altered to \(\mathrm{FA_{x}M A_{1-x}P b I_{3}}\) through \(\mathrm{MA^{+}}\) incorporation, \(\mathrm{MA^{+}}\) forms string interactions with the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{6}\) octahedra, thereby stabilizing \(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}\) against degradation into its photoinactive δ-FAPbI3 (yellow) phase.[103] In turn, \(\mathrm{FA^{+}}\) provides excellent thermal stability for the perovskite at temperatures over \(165\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (that \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) alone cannot achieve), through bonding strongly with \(\Gamma^{-}\) in the lattice.[102,103] However, \(\mathrm{FA^{+}}\) brings moisture sensitivity to the perovskite due to its higher hygroscopic nature,[102] and efforts have been made to provide stability against humidity by further incorporation of \({\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}\) with \(\mathrm{FA^{+}}\) or \(\mathrm{MA^{+}}\) in the perovskites. As such, \(\mathrm{Cs_{x}M A_{1-x}P b I_{3}}\) or \(\mathrm{Cs_{x}F A_{1-x}P b I_{3}}\) was reported to have considerably higher stability against humidity with contrast to the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) or \(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}\) ,[104] while for \(\mathrm{MA^{+}}\) -containing perovskites, \({\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}\) also contributed to thermal stability \(\sim\!120\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}^{[104,105]}\) and gave rise to highly cubic perovskites \((0.9\,\leq\,\mathrm{t}\,\leq\,1)\) when in \(\mathrm{FA^{+}}\) -based ones.[105] Due to these enhancements, \({\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}\) also enables less stringent conditions during the perovskite fabrication process.[106] In order to combine the desirable qualities of all three cations discussed above, a triple cation perovskite formula was
introduced next for Pb-only PSCs, in which the photoinactive \(\delta\mathrm{-FAPbI}_{3}\) phase was entirely inhibited by \({\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}\) , while more grain growth was facilitated.[106] With a more cubic structure, multication mixtures are regarded more stable, which has been proven by the triple cation perovskites showing remarkable high efficiencies and stabilities in Pb-PSCs.[106,107] With these fundamentals in mind, similar research on Pb-Sn perovskite absorbers is discussed in the following subsections. In Pb-only PSCs, incorporation of \(\mathrm{Rb^{+}}\) , \(\mathrm{K^{+}}\) , \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) etc.[108] was also reported to aid in defect passivation and stability; however, these cations have yet to be explored for Pb-Sn mixed perovskites.
## 3.2.2. Single A-Cation Pb-Sn Mixed Perovskites
Similar to \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) in Pb-PSCs, \(\mathrm{MAPb1}_{-}\) \(\mathrm{xSn}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) \((0\,\leq\,\mathrm{x}\,\leq\,1)\) is the widely studied in Pb-Sn PSCs, especially during the early stages. In 2014, \(\mathrm{MASn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) was used in a standard n-i-n device structure with mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) ETL, which resulted in \(4.18\%\) PCE.[23] Within the same year, the Jen group employed a planar inverted (p-i-n) architecture with a \(\mathrm{MAPb}_{0.85}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.15}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) absorber yielding a higher PCE of \(10.1\%\) .[12] The same group then employed PEDOT:PSS as ETL and fullerene derivatives as ETL in Pb-Sn mixed PSCs for first time, in the same year, to fabricate a ITO/ PEDOT:PSS/MA \(\mathrm{Pb}_{1-x}\mathrm{Sn}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{I}_{3}/\mathrm{PCBM}/\mathrm{bis-C}_{60}/\) Ag stack, concluding that the p-i-n configuration is better suited for Pb-Sn mixed
systems.[12] With \(\Gamma^{-}\) as the halide, various \(\mathbf{X}\) values in \(\mathrm{MAPb1}_{-}\mathrm{xSn}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) give rise to different crystal structures and in different PCEs[11] with the highest PCE of \(15.85\%\) (for \(\mathbf{x}=0.6\) ).[38] However, in 2017, the efficiency of MA-only Pb-Sn PSCs was improved up to \(17.63\%\) by substituting \(20\%\) of the halide site with \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) resulting in a \(\mathrm{MAPb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\left(\mathrm{I}_{0.8}\mathrm{Br}_{0.2}\right)\) formula.[9] So far, for the "standard" device architecture with \(\mathrm{MAPb}_{1-x}\mathrm{Sn}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) absorber, a highest PCE of \(15.5\%\) has been reported.[91]
High efficiencies are also seen with single cation FA-only Pb-Sn mixed PSCs (although not as widely reported as MA-only ones), where a highest PCE of \(17.25\%\) was reported for a device with a \(\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{NiO}_{\mathrm{x}}/\) \(\mathrm{FAPb}_{0.75}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.25}\mathrm{I}_{3}/\mathrm{PCBM}/\mathrm{bis-C}_{60}/\mathrm{Ag}\) stack,[8] and bandgap of \(1.36~\mathrm{eV}\) , very close to the ideal value for a single-function solar cell defined by the Shockley–Queisser limit \((1.35\,\mathrm{eV})\) .[68,109] Furthermore, in Pb-Sn mixed perovskites, a small amount of \({\mathrm{Sn}}^{2+}\) was found to aid stabilization of the \(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}\) \(\upalpha\) -phase (preventing the photoinactive \(\delta\mathrm{-FAPbI}_{3}\) phase; much like incorporation of \(\mathrm{MA^{+}}\) and \({\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}\) ), without the aid of any other A-site cation.[110] Reportedly, Sn incorporation further reduces the temperature necessary for the \(\delta\) -phase to \(\upalpha\) -phase conversion, to around \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , which otherwise lies in the range of \(\sim\!125\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) \(-\ 60\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .[102] In this study, high crystallinity and robustness were
achieved for a \(\mathrm{FAPb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) absorber, as a result of the higher structural stability.
The single cation systems discussed so far involve organic A sites, to fabricate inorganic–organic mixed perovskites. However, in Pb-Sn mixed PSCs, experimental reports on "all-inorganic" perovskites with \({\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{S}}^{+}\) as the single A-cation is not widely reported for PV applications. This is mostly due to implications of bandgap energies and phase stabilization issues. A study by Cao et al. reported a \(\mathrm{CsPb}_{1-x}\mathrm{Sn}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) perovskite with \(\mathbf{x}=0.5\) to absorb a wavelength of \(900\ \mathrm{nm}\) NIR-I radiation.[111] This result is in par with a recent theoretical study by Valadares et al.,[112] on \(\mathrm{CsPb}_{1-x}\mathrm{Sn}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) perovskites where most mixed compositions showed bandgap under \(1.2\ \mathrm{eV}\) with the lowest of \(0.984~\mathrm{eV}\) (for \(\mathbf{x}=0.8)\) . Thus, the all-inorganic \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed perovskites with \(\Gamma^{-}\) as halide may qualify for absorbing higher wavelengths of the solar spectrum; however, their \(\mathrm{Pb}\) -only and Sn-only counterparts have reported much lower efficiencies for \(\mathrm{CsPb}_{3}\) (majority of reports with \(\mathrm{PCE}\sim15\%\) with highest of \(18\%\) )[113] and \(\mathrm{CsSnI}_{3}\) (maximum PCE reported \(5.03\%\) despite the ideal bandgap of \(\sim\!1.3\ \mathrm{eV}\) and theoretical PCE of \(30\%\) ),[114] compared with their inorganic–organic mixed analogues. For \(\mathrm{CsPb}_{x}\mathrm{y}_{x}\) , the problem lies in larger bandgaps and unfavorable band alignments, as well as phase stabilization issues with the small \({\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{S}}^{+}\) cation radius.[113] In contrast, the smaller \({\mathrm{Sn}}^{2+}\) ion in \(\mathrm{CsSnX}_{3}\) is better equipped to accommodate \({\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{S}}^{+}\) in the perovskite lattice, with a tolerance factor closer to 1.[115] However, the poor interfacial and film qualities (which increase \({\mathrm{Sn}}^{2+}\) oxidation and non-radiative recombination), as well as phase stabilization issues under ambient conditions, have led to their low performance as well.[114] These limitations could be the primary reason for the lack of \(\mathrm{CsPb}_{1-x}\mathrm{Sn}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) compositions utilized for narrow-bandgap photovoltaic devices. However, as reported by Valadares et al.[112] in their theoretical simulations, \(\mathrm{CsPb}_{1-x}\mathrm{Sn}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) perovskites with atomic arrangements less susceptible to oxidation can be formed through low-temperature fabrication methods. As discussed in the previous section, in inorganic–organic mixed perovskites, incorporating \({\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{S}}^{+}\) is directly linked with improved stability of the absorber films. Similarly, all-inorganic \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed perovskites with a mixed IBr halide composition \((\mathrm{I};\mathrm{Br}=1;2)\) ) commonly report increased device stabilities under ambient conditions (although higher bandgap \(>\) \(1.6~\mathrm{eV}\) Br-rich perovskites are more suitable for wide-bandgap PSCs).[22,116] Liang et al. studied a \(\mathrm{CsPb}_{1-x}\mathrm{Sn}_{x}\mathrm{IBr}_{2}\) with \((\mathbf{x}=0.1\) and bandgap \(1.79\ \mathrm{eV})\) fabricated under ambient conditions without a glovebox or a thermal evaporator.[22] The authors reported a highly reproducible, stable, heat, and moisture resilient PSCs with a champion efficiency of \(11.33\%\) and a high \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}\) of \(1.27~\mathrm{eV}\) , with the potential for low-cost batch production. Li et al. studied the same perovskite composition with \(\mathbf{x}=0\) , 0.25, 0.5, and 0.75, which showed bandgap tunability from \(2.04~\mathrm{eV}\) to \(1.64~\mathrm{eV}\) with increasing \(\mathrm{Sn}\) .[116] For \(\mathbf{x}=0.25\) , a PCE of \(11.53\%\) with \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}=1.21\ \mathrm{V}\) was achieved with a better stability under illumination than its \(\mathrm{Pb}\) -only counterpart. A recent theoretical
study by Schwartz et al. on fully \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) containing \(\mathrm{CsPb}_{1-x}\mathrm{Sn}_{x}\mathrm{Br}_{2}\) compositions reported that the bandgap can be tuned 1.86 \((\mathbf{x}=1)\) ) to \(2.37~\mathrm{eV}\) \((\mathbf{x}=0)\) ) for applications in wide-bandgap PSCs or light-emitting diodes (LEDs).[117] More evidence is needed to realize fully inorganic Pb-Sn perovskites for PV applications.
## 3.2.3. Double A-Cation Pb-Sn Mixed Perovskites
Much like Pb-only PSCs, double cation systems have also been studied for Pb-Sn PSCs. Here, the perovskite precursor takes the form of \(\mathrm{FA_{1-y}M A_{y}P b_{1-x}S n_{x}I_{3}}\) , and to date, much of the higher efficiencies \((>\!20\%)\) for \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed PSCs has been reported commonly with \(\mathbf{x}=0.5–0.6\) and \(\mathrm{y}=0.3–0.5\) (see Table 1). Over \(20\%\) efficiencies were first reported in \(2019^{[34,39,40]}\) and higher efficiencies were continuously achieved[12,43,44] (currently over \(21\%\) ) with FAMA double cation system (recently addressing the surface passivation aspect more, to improve stability and PCE). Beyond the benefits of FA, MA mixed perovskites discussed for Pb-only PSCs above, the FA-MA mix in Pb-Sn mixed perovskites also aid in slower oxidation of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) ,[12] whilst enabling better quality films with uniform surface coverage. However, FA-based Pb-Sn perovskites are still sensitive to humidity, which may be a primary reason that surface passivation techniques provide prominent efficiency improvements in \(\mathrm{FA_{1-y}M A_{y}P b_{1-x}S n_{x}I_{3}}\) PSCs. Many of the reported high efficiencies were obtained by incorporating such passivation methods and other additives, which will be discussed in depth in Section 3.4.
have also been reported. Notably, for a \(\left(\mathrm{CsSnI}_{3}\right)_{0.3}(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3})_{0.6}\) formula, a \(15.8\%\) PCE was achieved by Zong et al. after incorporating a \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\) 3FACl Lewis-base adduct in the precursor solution,[118] through passivating the grain boundaries with \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\) (released following the evaporation of FACl during annealing), which accumulates at the grain boundaries. A remarkable retention of \(90\%\) of its initial PCE was reported for this device after \(288\,\mathrm{~h~}\) in ambient air. In recent reports, much higher \(\mathrm{SiE}\) of \(>~20\%\) (at maximum power point) was achieved with a \(\mathrm{Cs_{0.25}F A_{0.75}P b_{0.5}S n_{0.5}I_{3}}\) absorber, through a gas quenching process to replace anti-solvents in perovskite crystallization.[42] In this study, the PEDOT:PSS HTL was also replaced with PTAA, and the authors reported a high thermal stability of the cells with \(80\%\) original PCE retained after \(4000\,\mathrm{~h~}\) at \(85\%\) \(^{\circ}C,\) and fairly large are cells \((1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2})\) were fabricated with \(17.5\%\) PCE.
## 3.2.4. Triple A-Cation Pb-Sn Mixed Perovskites
Unlike their \(\mathrm{Pb}\) -only counterparts, only a limited number of triple Acation systems are reported for \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) PSCs. One such \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed triple cation, mixed halide system by Ji et al. utilized a \(\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.79}}\) \(\mathrm{MA}_{0.16}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.664}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.336}\mathrm{I}_{2.48}\mathrm{Br}_{0.52}\) perovskite,[37] following the A and X compositions reported for highly stable \(\mathrm{Pb}\) -only PSCs.[106] The authors fine-tuned the bandgap with a \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) stoichiometry of 0.336 to achieve a higher stability and a PCE of \(16\%\) PCE which retained \(93\%\) of its initial PCE for 30 days.[37] In other work, Bandara et al. reported a \(\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}(F A_{0.83}M A_{0.17})_{0.95}P b_{0.5}S n_{0.5}I_{3}}\) formula with \(50\%\) \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) content (and \(\Gamma\) only), resulting in \(12.04\%\) PCE.[47] Here, the authors employed toluene as the anti-solvent and a surface wash, to remove the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) dopants in the absorber. The same group later reported surface passivation with guanidinium bromide (GABr), improving the PCE up to \(14.4\%\) and reported \({\sim}83\%\) FF, the highest reported fill factor for \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed PSCs.[7] The authors found that in addition to the surface passivation, the perovskite was doped with \(\mathrm{Br}^{-}\) across the thickness, which gave rise to better electronic properties in the PSC. An important parameter for \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed triple cation perovskites, the effect of \({\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{s}}^{+}\) , was studied by Tosado et al. \(\mathrm{Cs_{x}(F A_{0.83}M A_{0.17})_{1-}}\) \(\mathrm{Pb}_{1-\mathrm{y}}\mathrm{Sn}_{\mathrm{y}}(\mathrm{I}_{0.83}\mathrm{Br}_{0.17})_{3}\) with \(\mathbf{x}=0.05\) , 0.10, 0.20 and \(\mathrm{y}=0\) , 0.25, 0.50, 0.75, 1.0.[119] Combining various x:y contents, the authors studied 15 different compositions for perovskite absorber grain morphologies, observing that the grain size was more correlated to the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) content than the \({\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{s}}^{+}\) content. However, it was noted that for larger \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) contents \((>\!75\%)\) , lower \({\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{s}}^{+}\) content was preferred for a pinholefree layer with large grains. Incorporation of \({\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{s}}^{+}\) pushed the Goldschmidt tolerance factor of these perovskites more into the cubic region further away from hexagonal (gradually from \(\mathbf{x}=0\) to \(\mathbf{x}=0.2\) ), aiding the stabilization of the perovskite against phase changes. Moreover, suppressing the yellow phase and the oxidation of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) , the \({\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{s}}^{+}\) incorporation was reported to give rise to highly crystalline perovskite films, and the authors fabricated \(75\%\) Sn-containing \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed PSC with \(11.05\%\) PCE with \(\mathbf{x}=0.05\) (such studies with \({>}60\%\) Sn incorporation is not widely reported in \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed PSCs yet). Best performance for \(50\%\) Sn and \(25\%\) Sn was achieved with \(\mathbf{x}=0.1\) with champion PCE of \(13.52\%\) and \(15.78\%\) , respectively. The devices retained \({\sim}80\%\) initial efficiency after 30 days under inert atmosphere.[119] More recently, Li et al. achieved \(20.4\%\) PCE for a \(\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.85}M A_{0.10}P b_{0.5}S n_{0.5}I_{3}}\) triple cation absorber.[41] Here, a 2-step fabrication method for the perovskite film as well as a 1D perovskite to passivate the surface was employed to achieve the high performance, which will be discussed in depth in
Section 3.4.3 of this review. However, the authors highlighted the importance of reduced MA content (only \(10\%\) of A-site) in reaching high stability in the devices, retaining \(92\%\) of initial PCE after continuous operation under 1 sun for \(450~\mathrm{h}\).
## 3.2.5. Engineering B and X sites of Pb-Sn Mixed Perovskites
As noted in Section 3.1, the B and X site molecular orbitals directly generate the \(\mathrm{VB}_{\mathrm{max}}\) and \(\mathrm{CB}_{\mathrm{min}}\) of the metal halide perovskite, thus altering B and X ions directly tunes the bandgap.[99,100] As discussed so far, the most common strategy for B-site tuning is \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) substitution to completely or partially replace \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) (Figure 10a-c),[98] the bandgap shows a "bowing effect" with increasing Sn at via B-site and will be discussed later in this section. When engineering the X site halide to alter the bandgap, among available halides, \(\mathrm{Br}^{-}\) incorporation in \(\Gamma\) only perovskites has been widely acknowledged to increases the bandgap (Figure 10d,e),[32] where the \(\mathrm{Br}^{-}\) concentration was reported to directly correlate with the bandgap increment in \(\mathrm{Pb}\) -only perovskites, following a quadratic relationship.[32] Furthermore, it improves crystallinity, increases grain sizes, and passivates defects while enhancing charge extraction, reducing disorder and hysteresis, and providing ambient stability.[9]
better energy-level alignments with the charge transporting layers of the PSC devices fabricated. Compared to the \(12.09\%\) PCE of the reference, an improved \(14.71\%\) PCE was observed for the improved absorber, which was further improved to \(17.13\%\) by increasing the thickness of the absorber. In another study, a triple cation \(\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.79}M A_{0.16}P b_{1-x}S n_{x}I_{2-x}8B r_{0.52}}\) was employed to achieve Pb-Sn mixed PSCs with champion PCE up to \(16.10\%\) with good stability and negligible hysteresis.[37] A comparison with a Pb-only counterpart was not discussed in this study, and the authors seem to have adopted the formula directly from triple cation mixed halide Pb-only perovskites given their popularity in highly stable and efficient PSCs.[37] In a similar study for a \(\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.79}M A_{0.16}P b_{0.52}S n_{0.513}}\) perovskite, \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) was incorporated as post-treatment on the original I-containing perovskite film.[37] Here, the authors have employed a GABr (guanidinium bromide) treatment as a surface passivation layer for the absorber, which resulted in a distribution of \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) ions through the bulk of the perovskite film, with a final film composition of \(\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.79}M A_{0.16}P b_{0.5}S n_{0.5}I_{0.24}B r_{0.6}}\). An increment of the PCE from \(12.2\%\) ( \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\) only PSC) to \(14.4\%\) with high fill factors of \(83\%\) \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) incorporated devices) was reported in this study. Similar to previous reports, the bandgap increased from \(1.25~\mathrm{eV}\) to \(1.30~\mathrm{eV}\) with Br- incorporation, while the \(\mathrm{CB}_{\mathrm{min}}\) and \(\mathrm{VB}_{\mathrm{max}}\) were shifted downwards by \(0.1~\mathrm{eV}\) with respect to the reference energy levels. The authors also reported a shift in Fermi energy of the \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) incorporated absorber by \(0.1~\mathrm{eV}\) upwards (with respect to the reference perovskite Fermi level) toward CB edge, signifying that the \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) incorporation also contributes toward n-doping of the perovskite film. This can especially be beneficial for Pb-Sn mixed perovskites in compensating for loses due to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation, thus should be further explored in Sn-containing PSCs. A remarkable PCE of \(19.03\%\) was achieved by Li et al. for \(\mathrm{F_{A0.6}M A_{0.4}P b_{0.4}S n_{0.6}(I_{1-x}B r_{x})_{3}}\) composition with \(\mathbf{x}=0.06\) compared to the \(17.03\%\) of \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\) only reference.[120] The surface recombination was reported to reduce thorough this route, with the defect density being reduced from \(10^{17}\) to \(10^{15}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\). \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) incorporation was found to aid in passivating the grain boundaries which contribute largely to the leakage currents of the Pb-Sn mixed PSCs.[120]
For Pb-based PSCs, \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) incorporation at the X site has also been reported in some instances. However, the effect of it is not very well understood, despite the improved film morphology and slow crystallization rate reported.[121] Similarly, \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) incorporation can be seen in a limited number of Pb-Sn mixed PSC studies.[12,82] In one of the first reported studies on Pb-Sn mixed PSCs by Zuo et al,[12] a \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) containing \(\mathrm{MAPb1}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Sn}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{I}_{3-\mathrm{y}}\mathrm{Cl}_{\mathrm{y}}\) perovskite was explored in comparison with its Pb-only counterpart \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3-\mathrm{y}}\mathrm{Cl}_{\mathrm{y}}\). A PCE of \(10.1\%\) was reported for the Pb-Sn mixed PSCs with \(15\%\) Sn incorporation in the above formula, compared to a \(7.3\%\) of the Pb-only PSC for inverted devices. The authors report a steady loss of \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) in both Pb-only and Pb-Sn perovskites during the fabrication process and attributed it to a similar process as the mechanism of iodide loss in pure organo-lead iodide perovskites (via decomposition of MAI). The authors suggested that MACl was decomposed into methylamine \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)\) and HCl gases, during the film annealing process. In addition, a separate \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) containing \(\mathrm{MAPb}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Sn}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Cl}_{3}\) phase was observed in both perovskites under XRD, with a strong suppression of this phase with Sn substitution. The authors reported the formation of Sn-Cl bonds before the other bonds in the system, indicating the influence of the presence of Sn in \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) containing perovskites. This rapid formation of \(\mathrm{Sn-Cl}\) was observed to complicate the growth and resultant morphology of Pb-Sn mixed
perovskites, limiting the degree of Sn substitution. Furthermore, in contrast to the \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) containing perovskites, the unit cell structure for \(\mathrm{MAPb}_{0.85}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.15}\mathrm{I}_{3-\mathrm{y}}\mathrm{Cl}_{\mathrm{y}}\) was found to be tetragonal (similar to \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3-\mathrm{y}}\mathrm{Cl}_{\mathrm{y}}\)),[12] where \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\)-containing Pb-Sn mixed perovskites are often reported to be cubic.[27] However, the authors emphasized the need for more research toward understanding the function of \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) in enhancing the ultimate material properties of perovskites. In another Pb-Sn mixed study by Liu et al, \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\)-incorporated \(\mathrm{MASn}_{3-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Cl}_{3-\mathrm{x}}\) formula was reported to result in high-quality film morphology and low bandgap, with an optical absorption edge exceeding \(1000~\mathrm{nm}\).[82] Similar to the previous study by Zuo et al, [12] the \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) content here was reported to reduce during the perovskite film fabrication process; however, the authors suggested the loss mechanism as sublimation of MACl.[82] Here, an accelerated MACl sublimation was reported to result in films with better film coverage, crystallinity, and reduced trap density, which was achieved by a vacuum-assisted thermal annealing process. However, the authors reported a resulting composition of \(\mathrm{MASn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) where negligible amounts of \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) were observed in the film. Moreover, incomplete sublimation of MACl resulting from annealing the film in non-vacuum environments was reported to adversely affect the perovskite, acting as an impurity. Thus, the functionality of \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) for the perovskite functionality is not very clear in this study as well. In later reports, high efficiency up to \(18.4\%\) was reported with \(2.5\%~\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) incorporation in a \(\mathrm{F_{A0.6}M A_{0.4}P b_{0.4}S n_{0.6}(I_{1-x}B r_{x})_{3}}\) perovskite by Zhao et al.[122] The improvements were attributed to increased grain sizes, better crystallinity, grain boundary passivation, high charge carrier lifetimes, and high carrier mobilities. So far, majority of the Pb-Sn PSCs seem to prefer \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\) only compositions over mixed halides. However, considering the above results, mixed halide systems are another viable pathway for future exploration in Pb-Sn mixed systems en route to achieving higher PCE.
Considering the B-site, as noted earlier in this section, when tuning bandgap \((1.2\mathrm{~-~}1.3\mathrm{~eV})\) by altering \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}{:}\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) ratio, the bandgap variation shows a "bowing effect" with increasing \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) content, defying Vergard's law (Figure 11a).[10] Here, a decrement of the bandgap is initially seen with increasing Sn content, reaching a minimum at \({\sim}40\%\ -\ 60\%\ \mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) (the perovskite formula decides the exact Sn content to achieve the minimum bandgap) which then increases when \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) content is further increased.[10] This bowing effect can be described using Equation 3 below, where \(\mathrm{E_{g}}\) is the bandgap energy and b is the bowing parameter. This describes how strong the bandgap bowing is.
\[
\mathrm{G}(x)=(1-x)\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{g}}(x=0)+x\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{g}}(x=1)-\mathrm{b}x(1-x)
\]
With the bandgap, both \(\mathrm{VB}_{\mathrm{max}}\) and \(\mathrm{CB}_{\mathrm{min}}\) were observed to shift, and the reasoning for the bowing effect is still under debate (Figure 11b).[10] Shift toward lower energies (higher wavelengths) of absorption edges in EQE (also known as PCE, incident photon conversion efficiency) and optical absorption spectra (Figure 11c and d), as well as the PL emission peaks,[68,123] was seen with Sn incorporation of up to \(50\%\) which then show opposite shifts upon reaching \(100\;\mathrm{Sn\%}\).
and I(5p) orbitals contribute to the \(\mathrm{VB}_{\mathrm{max}}\) through \(\upsigma\) -antibonding interactions, while the \(\mathrm{CB}_{\mathrm{min}}\) is made from a mixture of \(\upsigma\) - antibonding molecular orbitals from \(\mathrm{Pb}(6\mathrm{p})\) and I(5s) and \(\pi\) - antibonding molecular orbitals from \(\mathrm{Pb}(6\mathrm{p})\) and I(5p).[99] It is generally accepted that the band structure and bandgap of Sn-based perovskites originate in a similar manner through Sn and I atomic orbitals.[123] However, ab initio calculations at DFT, hybrid DFT, and QSGW levels of theory have shown, via analysis of the partial density of states (PDOS) (Figure 11e),[123] that the atomic orbitals of Sn (right) reside at more shallower energy levels than Pb orbitals (left), resulting in more shallower \(\mathrm{VB}_{\mathrm{max}}\) and \(\mathrm{CB}_{\mathrm{min}}\) energy levels for Sn. Thus, for a mixed Pb-Sn system (Figure 11e (middle)), the orbital diagram would show a \(\mathrm{CB}_{\mathrm{min}}\) made of Pb-I orbitals and a \(\mathrm{VB}_{\mathrm{max}}\) made of Sn-I orbitals which gives rise to the anomalous bandgap with a bowing parameter of \(1.08~\mathrm{eV}\) . This research also suggests that the bowing effect is related primarily to the energy mismatch between the Pb 6s/6p and the Sn 5s/ 5p states, suggesting that spin-orbit coupling and the short range ordering are not the primary cause of the bowing. Calculations which include and exclude spin-orbit coupling found very similar bowing parameters, although the linear term of the bandgap as a function of Sn content is strongly affected due to the lower atomic number of Sn relative to Pb. The short range
ordering of Pb and Sn atoms is considered to not influence the bandgap bowing, as random distributions of Pb and Sn atoms were shown to result in a lower total energy than clustered distributions of Pb and Sn atoms, suggesting that the formation of Pb and Sn clusters is not preferred. The results of this research also show that lower levels of theory, such as GGA level functionals, describe the behavior of the band edges and bandgap bowing well, which is useful for keeping calculation of the large systems needed for these solid solution calculations affordable.
The role of X site atomic orbitals in band edge formation explains how the bandgap can be tuned via halide ions, as discussed above. In Section 3.1, it was also noted that the A-site can indirectly affect the bandgap. This can happen as a result of either perovskite lattice contraction or tilting of \(\mathrm{BX}_{6}\) octahedra.[3] For instance, it was reported that incorporating \({\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}\) in FA-based Pb-Sn perovskites rich in Pb increases the bandgap via octahedral tilting, and when rich in Sn, this decreased the bandgaps through lattice contraction.[3] In pure Pb-based \(\mathrm{FA}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Cs}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) , DFT calculations have shown that the bandgap increases with increasing \({\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}\) content, as the small ionic radius of \({\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}\) induces a lattice contraction. Due to the relatively large size of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) , and the size mismatch between \(\mathrm{FA^{+}}\) and \({\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}\) , this causes a lowering of the crystal symmetry from the cubic to
tetragonal phase, which causes a decreased orbital overlap of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) and \(\Gamma^{-}\)in the band edges, causing a lower band dispersion, and thus an increased bandgap.[126,127]
## 3.3. Fabrication of Absorber Layers
In Pb-Sn PSCs, the absorber layer is a thin film of a few hundred nanometres, and the fabrication of very high-quality film tuning its structure and morphology has been continuously regarded as the primary path in achieving highly efficient and stable PSCs.[63,128] In other words, a highly crystalline, pinhole free, smooth film with large grains is desirable, which can generally be tuned via the selection of suitable fabrication methods, processing conditions, surface post-treatments, compositional engineering, etc.[128,129] These parameters are interdependent, and the perovskites (especially Sn-incorporated ones) are highly sensitive to small alterations in them, which makes it difficult to
independently identify the impacts of each factor on the reported changes in most cases.[63]
## 3.3.1. Fabrication Methods
The perovskite film can be fabricated through a solution-based deposition or vacuum deposition,[129] the former being most widely reported with the lower cost associated and ease of adoption in commercial scale. In laboratory scale, spin coating is commonly used, where the perovskite precursors are dissolved in a suitable solvent/solvent mix and deposited either through one-step deposition, or multi-step (sequential) deposition.[129] Although the one-step process is straight forward, cost-effective, and more suited for planar device architectures compared to sequential deposition, the latter provides better morphological and crystallization control and is less prone to pinhole formation. In one report employing sequential deposition, a two-step bilayer
interdiffusion growth process (BIG process) was employed by Li et al,[41] to produce very high-quality \(\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{MA}_{0.1}\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) absorbers (Figure 12a-e) with large grains and reduced amount of volatile MA. However, this processing route requires additional annealing steps to either aid crystallization, merge the precursor layers, or remove excess solvent accumulation as well as controlled atmospheric conditions in order to aid proper crystallization.[129] To achieve better control in a one-step deposition process, the precursor solution has to be carefully fine-tuned considering the solvent system, as well as the precursor concentration and ratios.[63,129] Considering fabrication methods, when it comes to the large area manufacturing of PSCs the spin coating method has limitations. As a resolution to this, another method of solution deposition, blade coating, was recently adapted for a Pb-Sn mixed perovskite by Li et al.[130] This method was developed as means of fabricating high-quality large area perovskite films, where the precursor solution was coated in a one-step process and subjected to both vacuum and thermal annealing (Figure 12f).[130,131] The authors fabricated Pb-Sn mixed PSCs with \(1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\) active area using this method.[130] Also, this method was used by the authors for producing for Pb-only perovskite solar modules with \(10.08\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\) active areas (4 cells connected in series).[131] More large area methods such as these need to be further explored for Pb-Sn mixed PSCs.
The next type of fabrication is vacuum deposition of precursors. Although this method fabricates highly uniform and crystalline perovskite films,[132] the evaporation rate must be heavily controlled for each precursor/precursor mixture to achieve a stable stoichiometry throughout the films. In some cases, vacuum techniques are used
in sequence with solution processing, where a vacuum deposited film of the first precursor is exposed to the vapor of the second precursor allowing better control of the diffusion of the two precursors.[133] Although vacuum techniques are not suited for low-cost applications, they have been investigated for perovskite-Si tandem devices. There are many other factors affecting the quality and optoelectronic properties of perovskite thin films, including the environmental conditions during fabrication (air exposure, humidity, etc.), annealing conditions (temperature, time, controlled solvent annealing, etc.), as well as solvents and additives.[63] These are of higher importance in Pb-Sn and Sn-incorporated perovskites with higher reactivity (as discussed in the following sections), thus must be properly engineered.
## 3.3.2. Environmental Conditions
Most perovskites are fabricated in an inert environment due to their air and moisture sensitivity (mostly due to \(\mathrm{FA^{+}}\)).[128,129] However, this is even more critical in Sn-containing perovskites, due to the rapid oxidation of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) upon exposure to atmosphere.[64] Interestingly, in come \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) containing Pb-only perovskites, film fabrication under \({\sim}30\%\) humidity was reported to give rise to higher efficiencies through the formation of larger grains, and achieving full, pinhole-free surface coverage.[134] However, Pb-Sn perovskites still suffer rapid oxidation of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) even when processed under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) glove box with carefully controlled moisture levels. Very recent reports have provided viable methods to tackle this, such as the incorporation of metallic Sn powder in Pb-Sn mixed perovskites (further discussed in Section 3.4.2).[45] However, in the future it is best to employ proper encapsulation and surface passivation of the Pb-Sn mixed PSCs, to protect the films against ambient moisture and air.
## 3.3.3. Solvent and Additive Engineering
In solution-processed perovskites, the solvent system must be well engineered to allow compact, pinhole-free, highly crystalline, and morphologically controlled perovskite films. The most widely used solvent systems for Pb-Sn mixed perovskite precursor solutions contain a mixture of polar aprotic solvents DMF (N,N-dimethylformamide) and DMSO (dimethyl sulfoxide).[129] DMSO in the solvent system reportedly allows controlled and complete conversion of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) into the perovskite film by formation of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-DMSO adducts, while when DMF was used alone, \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) crystallization lacks control.[135] Similarly, \(\mathrm{SnI}_{2}\)-DMSO complexes adducts were reported for Sn-based perovskites as well.[29,136] Although these solvent systems aid in bypassing the limitations for homogenous nucleation in one-step fabrication processes,[129] the toxicity of these solvents limits their usage in large-scale commercial applications, while recent reports in Sn-only perovskites also suggest that DMSO can contribute to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation.[137] Therefore, testing more suitable solvent systems is highly important for Pb-Sn perovskites at present.
Another popular technique for high-quality perovskites is the "antisolvent treatment",[138] which is commonly used in high-performing PSCs.[139] This technique bypasses the limitations of extremely slow solvent evaporation rates and facilitates fast crystallization of perovskites.[129] The anti-solvent is selected such that it is well miscible with the solvents but does not take part in dissolving the perovskite or
its precursors. During the spin coating, the anti-solvent is dripped on to the film to cause instantaneous local supersaturation.[138] This in turn gives rise to the fast heterogeneous nucleation process. Inspired by the Pb-only PSC fabrication methods, several different anti-solvents have been explored for Pb-Sn mixed perovskites (Figure 13a). Among these, the most common are toluene,[11,128] anisole,[1,13] chlorobenzene,[17,88] and diethyl ether,[122,140] while less commonly, other anti-solvents such as ethyl acetate[34] and o-xylene[91] have also been tested for Pb-Sn mixed PSCs. Although the effect of different anti-solvents on Pb-only perovskites has been thoroughly investigated,[138,139] similar studies for Pb-Sn perovskites are not very widely reported. However, the anti-solvent may affect other important parameters in Sn-based perovskites such as the unwanted \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) content in the films, as reported by Bandara et al,[47] in a study on the effect of three different anti-solvents on a \(\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{FA}_{0.79}\mathrm{MA}_{0.16}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{S}_{3}\) triple cation perovskite (Figure 13a–c). Among the anti-solvents, toluene was shown to reduce the unwanted \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) content produced as a result of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation of the films, improving the efficiency to \(11.62\%\) from \(6.98\%\) for chlorobenzene and \(6.38\%\) for anisole. This is due to the non-polar nature of toluene, which aids in removing the non-polar by-products formed with the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) dopants (such as \(\mathrm{SnI}_{4}\)). In addition to this, different additives such as ascorbic acid or \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\)-based additives have been especially proven to aid stability against the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation.[25,141] These additives can be included either in the anti-solvent, or added directly into the precursor solution. However, some additives such as \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\) treatments[7,41,142] are used as post-treatments to improve perovskite surfaces and are further discussed in later sections.
Other high-quality Pb-Sn perovskite films have been fabricated by additional techniques such as gas blowing techniques[42,143] and vacuum treatment.[138,139,144] One recent study by Werner et al[142] employed gas quench processing by \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) gas blowing during the perovskite spin coating stage. The authors highlighted the importance of an alternate method to the physical anti-solvent treatment, given the need for extreme precision in the latter technique where the anti-solvent purity, drip timing and placement, glovebox conditions, surrounding atmosphere, and the technical skills of the experimenter have to be consistent to achieve high-quality films.[42] Using a \(\mathrm{FA}_{0.75}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.25}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{S}_{3}\) perovskite, the authors achieved \(19\%\) stabilized efficiency from \(0.06\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\) and \(17.8\%\) from \(1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\) solar cells using the gas quenching method, achieving better film quality than the anti-solvent method irrespective of external factors such as glove box conditions. In other work employing vacuum treatment to replace anti-solvents, Nejad et al [144] reported efficiency increments from \(15.4\%\) for anti-solvent (chlorobenzene) treated PSCs up to \(18.2\%\) for \(10\ \mathrm{s}\) vacuum annealed PSCs containing \(\mathrm{MA}_{0.2}\mathrm{FA}_{0.8}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{S}_{3}\) absorber (Figure 13d-h). \(10\ \mathrm{s}\) vacuum treatment was found to be the best out of 2, 5, 10, and \(30\ \mathrm{s}\) vacuum times, with grain sizes reaching \(2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) for \(10\ \mathrm{s}\) compared to \(<500\ \mathrm{nm}\) for anti-solvent treatment.[144]
## 3.3.4. Annealing Conditions
Thermal annealing aids perovskite crystallization from the precursors by removing excess solvent and completing grain growth.[128,129] Thus, the annealing temperature and time as well as other conditions (such as solvent-assisted vapor annealing), morphological control of the perovskite film can be achieved.[63] The temperature must be high enough to fully crystallize the film; however, it should allow all the
precursor components to withstand without decomposing, and an optimum annealing duration should be employed to complete the crystallization process.[146] The perovskite composition and thickness decide these parameters, thus should be optimized suitably.[128,129] Generally for Pb-Sn mixed perovskites, an annealing temperature in the range of \(70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}^{[1,42,88]}\) and time of \(5~\mathrm{min}\) to \(30\ \mathrm{min}^{[24,37,144]}\) are suitable, the most widely reported being \(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(10\ \mathrm{min}^{[34,40,130]}\) In some Pb-only PSCs, two-step annealing processes (high-temperature rapid annealing followed by low-temperature brief heating) were explored[147] which improved film morphology resulting in better device performances. This method can also be seen in the more recent studies for Pb-Sn mixed PSCs[95,142] Another approach is the "solvent-assisted vapour annealing" where the film is annealed under an environment of saturated solvent (such as DMF) vapor.[148] In this method, the precursor molecules are reorganized with the aid of the solvent vapor that diffuses into the perovskite to result in a high-quality
perovskite film. Although this method is commonly seen in sequentially deposited Pb-based perovskites[148] and not yet widely used for Pb-Sn mixed perovskites, a process similar to this method was adapted by Leijtens et al., for Pb-Sn mixed perovskites using MACl vapor (onestep fabrication of \(\mathrm{FA}_{0.75}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.25}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{3}\)) to aid grain growth and heal cracks in the films.[13] The researchers followed previous studies in Pb-based PSCs,[149] where the methylaniline \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)\) gas-induced defect healing was first reported through the formation of a perovskite-\(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) intermediate during the annealing process. Leijtens et al. coupled this method with acidifying the precursor solution using formic acid, to improve the PSC efficiency from \(13.1\%\) (of thermally annealed film) to \(15.6\%\) with micron-sized grains and increases in device \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}\) and FF.[13]
Jiang et al. employed a delayed annealing process for \(\mathrm{MA}_{0.4}\mathrm{FA}_{0.6}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.4}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.6}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) perovskites, whereby the fabricated films (toluene anti-solvent) were simply kept in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) glove box for \(15~\mathrm{min}\)
before annealing at \(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\), achieving \(18.68\%\) efficiency compared to the \(14.47\%\) of reference without the delayed anneal.[150] The improvements were attributed to the slow evaporation of DMSO solvent in the perovskite films with delayed annealing, improving mass transport and diffusion, giving rise to better film quality (more uniform, compact, and pinhole-free layers with reduced surface roughness).[150]
## 3.3.5. Surface Post-treatments
It was noted in Section 3.3.3 that some additives are used on the perovskite films as a post-treatments. The post-treatments materials are often spin-coated on the perovskite absorber right after its fabrication and annealing in order to facilitate surface passivation (to reduce trap density and thus alleviate non-radiative recombination and extend charge carrier recombination lifetimes),[151] provide protection for the perovskite layer against degradation,[152] dope the perovskite to improve the charge transport[153] and to facilitate larger crystal growth via Ostwald ripening.[154] In Pb-Sn mixed perovskites, the surface treatments are regarded of especial benefit, to hinder the surface \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation by suppressing interaction with adsorbed or atmospheric \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\).
In Pb-only perovskites, post-treatments using guanidinium \((\mathrm{CH}_{6}\mathrm{N}_{3}^{+};\mathrm{~\boldmath~\cal~G~}\mathrm{A}^{+})\) halides are employed for passivation of traps,[153,155,156] to induce Ostwald ripening of grains,[154] to increase the carrier lifetimes, and to increase stability and defect tolerance of the devices by acting as a dopant or alloy.[153,157] Similarly, Sn-only perovskites reported PCE reaching \(10\%\) using \(\mathrm{GA^{+}}\) in \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskites doped with \(1\%\) \(\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}\) (ethylenediammonium diiodide) obtaining \(9.6\%\) (after storing in an inert environment for \(2000\ \mathrm{h}\)).[158] The effect of \(\mathrm{GA^{+}}\) salts in Pb-Sn mixed perovskites has also been reported a few times in very recent studies[17,39] and has proven to be highly effective, thus will be discussed in depth in Section 3.4.3. Other materials based on Lewis-base post-treatments have been reported for Sn-incorporated perovskites, with organic molecules such as ethane-1,2-diamine (edamine) to suppress recombination.[159] More simple methods such as a post solvent wash with a non-polar solvent can be incorporated in the fabrication process to remove the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) dopants on the Pb-Sn mixed perovskites to further suppress the interfacial recombination.[47] Another method yielding high PCEs in Pb-Sn mixed PSCs is the fabrication of a 2D perovskite to act as a protective barrier at the surface, passivating grain boundaries.[130,160] This technique is discussed further in Section 3.4.4.
## 3.4. Toward Higher Performance of Pb-Sn Mixed PSCs
## 3.4.1. Suppression of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) Oxidation
As discussed so far, the most prominent issue that limits the maximum performance of Pb-Sn mixed PSCs, the instability of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\).[30,31] \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) is the thermodynamically more stable ionic form of Sn, which deems it greatly sensitive to air and moisture, and readily oxidizes as given in Equation 4.
\[
\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\rightarrow\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}+2e^{-}
\]
When in the perovskite matrix, this oxidation process produces Sn vacancies which deteriorate the lattice, while the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) ions behave as p
type dopants (holes) in the perovskite.[141,161,162] This hole generation process, known as "self-doping," can be expressed as per below (Equation 5).[141]
\[
\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}+\mathrm{O}_2\rightarrow\mathrm{SnO}_2+2\mathrm{h}^+
\]
These dopants become trap sites for mobile charge carriers, contributing largely to non-radiative recombination in Sn-containing perovskites.[30,163] Thus, Sn-containing perovskites mostly turn out to be p-type due to the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) doping, and the resulting high concentration of p-type carriers contributes to scattering, in turn reducing the charge carrier mobility.[122]
This persistent phenomenon has caused the Pb-Sn perovskite community to focus widely on defect passivation and inhibiting the oxidation process, which has become key in PCE and stability improvements in these PSCs.[164] The vast majority of Pb-Sn PSCs are processed in controlled environments in the absence of oxygen and moisture, and proper encapsulation is critical for device testing in ambient conditions.[141] Incorporation of \(\geq\,50\%\) of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) in Pb-Sn PSCs was reported to significantly decrease the tin oxidation process,[31] as has various additives incorporated in the precursor, the most common being \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\).[14,31,141] Compared to the typical halides used in metal halide perovskites, \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\)anion has a much smaller radius \((129\ \mathrm{pm})\),[98] too small to inhabit the perovskite crystal lattice, thus segregates at the grain boundaries.[64] Therefore, \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\) does not alter the bandgap, but improves the Pb-Sn perovskite absorber[64,165] when incorporated in small amounts (generally optimized to \(20\%\) of overall \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) concentration, with further increments \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\) amounts becoming detrimental to the PSC performance).[45] Several adduct forms of \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\) (such as \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\mathrm{-DMSO}^{[166]}\) or \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\mathrm{-3FACl}^{[18]}\)) were also reported for Pb-Sn PSCs, which aided in trap density reduction resulting in enhanced charge transport through the device.
Other additives in oxidation mitigation include \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\)-pyrazine complexes,[167] ascorbic acid[25] (antioxidants), \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{2}\) acid (reducing agent),[33] and MASCN.[168] Additives such as ascorbic acid have better antioxidant capabilities than \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\),[25] which could further retard the carrier recombination of Pb-Sn mixed perovskites, improving long-term stability. It was also reported that the perovskite behaves as n-type under the presence of a reducing agent, through successfully suppressing the formation of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\),[33] whereas \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)from additives such as MASCN form strong interactions with \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) preventing its interaction with \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\).[168]
Taking these efforts into consideration, a recent study by Mundt et al[169] investigated the origination of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation and phenomena that govern the process. As the authors point out, the oxidation process occurs not only when the Pb-Sn mixed perovskites are exposed to the atmosphere, but also when the films age inside an inert environment with heavily controlled \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and moisture levels (although film degradation under atmospheric conditions is much more rapid due to higher amounts of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and moisture). Employing a \(\mathrm{MA}_{0.4}\mathrm{FA}_{0.6}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.4}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.6}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) perovskite absorber, the authors studied the bulk and surface/interface regions of the film upon inert \(\left(\mathrm{N}_{2}\right)\) conditions and under air, at RT and \(60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).[169] In all cases, the results strongly suggest that the bulk crystal structure remains stable under aging, even when subjected to combined external stresses such as heat, electrical bias, and light. Thus, the authors conclude the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation process affects PSC device performance at the interfaces, introducing surface barriers at the perovskite surface to inhibit efficient charge extraction.
The authors further point out the contribution of ionic migration, specifically of \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\), which will act as a driving force to the oxidation process, such that it occurs spontaneously even in the absence of other external stresses. Even under inert conditions, the inherent \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)in the perovskite as well as small amounts of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) or moisture present inside the glovebox, the precursors and solvents will contribute to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation and the formation of species such as \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), and undercoordinated metal cations \(\mathrm{Sn}^{6<2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}^{6<2+}\) on the surface.[169] While the authors suggest this phenomenon can be
affected by a variety of different factors including the chemical composition of the perovskite, this study points out the need for proper surface passivation methods in Pb-Sn mixed perovskite absorbers which will be discussed in the next sections of this review.
## 3.4.2. Removal and Reduction of \(S n^{4+}\)
As discussed above, there have been a significant amount of effort toward retarding the oxidation of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\); however, much attention has not been given toward tackling the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) amount in the perovskite which may arise from the \(\mathrm{SnI}_{2}\) precursor itself or during the film formation. On the one hand, non-polar anti-solvents such as toluene have been reported to remove some of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) coming from the precursor solution, during the fabrication of the films.[47] On the other hand, some researchers have progressed a step further and succeeded in converting the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) back into \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) within the Pb-Sn mixed perovskite solution using metallic Sn powder as a reducing agent (Equation 6),[34,40] inspired by similar work carried out in Sn-only perovskites.[170]
\[
\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}+\mathrm{Sn}\longrightarrow2\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}
\]
As seen in Figure 14a(left), a regular Pb-Sn mixed perovskite precursor solution takes a yellow color, which, upon exposing to atmospheric conditions, rapidly changes color to take a red hue indicating the oxidation of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\).[34] For a \(\mathsf{M A}_{0.3}\mathsf{F A}_{0.7}\mathsf{P b}_{0.5}\mathsf{S n}_{0.5}\mathsf{I}_{3}\) perovskite solution, Lin et al. incorporated a small amount of metallic Sn powder \((5~\mathrm{mg~ml^{-1}})\) which not only prevented the oxidation of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) (Figure 14a(right)), but also reduced the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) changing the red solutions back to yellow. This method paves way for the fabrication of Pb-Sn perovskite films in air and improves the device stability. Here, Lin et al. achieved the highest reported PCE at the time of \(21.1\%\) ( \(19.5\%\) certified) for Pb-Sn mixed single-junction PSCs.[34] Interestingly, compared to the reference PSC, the incorporation of metallic Sn also affected the
trends in performance parameters with film thickness (Figure 14b), giving superior stability in FF irrespective the thickness, and an increase in \(\mathrm{J_{SC}}\) (as well as the PCE) with increasing thickness (up to \(860~\mathrm{nm}\)). In general, better reproducibility of results was achieved with the incorporation of Sn powder, due to the reduction of the Sn vacancies in the perovskite lattice, and increasing the charge carrier diffusion length up to \(3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\).[34] In a similar study by Jiang et al,[40] Sn powder was incorporated in a \(\mathsf{M A}_{0.4}\mathsf{F A}_{0.6}\mathsf{P b}_{0.4}\mathsf{S n}_{0.6}\mathsf{I}_{3}\) perovskite formula, to report another remarkable PCE of \(20.7\%\). The authors attributed the high
performance to the increased charge carrier lifetimes \(\sim700\) ns and higher fill factors of \(80.34\%\) due to reduced trap density and recombination.[40] The study also suggested a slight improvement of the surface quality of the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) reduced films.
Despite the high performance reported by Lin et al., the authors suggested more efforts via device interface and grain passivation in order to achieve higher \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}\) and FF values.[34] Thus, the same research group attempted a surface-anchoring restirterionic antioxidant for the same perovskite composition, reporting the next highest PCE of \(21.7\%\) \((20.7\%\) certified) a year later (Figure 14c-f).[45] This method further inhibited the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation and passivated the surface and grain boundaries, giving rise to the higher PCE, stability, and uniformity of the devices. Here, a small amount of formamidine sulfinic acid (FSA), a strong reducing agent with a stable zwitterionic configuration, was used in the precursor solution to simultaneously serve multiple functionalities. Although FSA had no effect on the crystallization or film morphology, it significantly suppressed the oxidation on the solid film surface after exposure to air and reduced surface \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) by anchoring on the surface and grain boundaries (Figure 14c,d). Furthermore, passivating both electron and hole vacancies in the lattice was achieved due to the zwitterionic nature of FSA, largely reducing the trap density of the \(\mathrm{MA}_{0.3}\mathrm{FA}_{0.7}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) absorber, decreasing non-radiative recombination, increasing charge carrier lifetimes, and giving rise to a spatially uniform film (uniform bandgap) which resulted in a higher PCE and better reproducibility (Figure 14e,f).[45] At the time of this review, this report contains the best-certified efficiency value for single-junction Pb-Sn mixed PSC. However, it is worthwhile noting that, to achieve high performance through alleviating \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) issue and passivating the surface, \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\), metallic Sn powder, and FSA zwitterion were all employed in the precursor solution here. This is a clear indication of the efforts that must be taken to tackle the oxidation of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\), and the magnitude of the challenge in reaching the maximum potential of Pb-Sn mixed perovskites. Thus, more efforts toward understanding the retention or removal of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) are still needed in Pb-Sn mixed perovskites.
## 3.4.3. Surface Passivation with Hetero Structures
As discussed previously, high-quality perovskite films are the key to high-performing PSCs. Generally, for Sn-containing perovskites, the majority of Sn oxidation happens on the surface of the perovskite; thus, a suitable surface passivation technique should be used to protect the perovskite from degradation and improve the film quality.[171] Some theoretical studies on Sn-only \(\mathrm{CsSnI}_{3}\) perovskite suggest the use of (1) low-dimensional Sn perovskites (such as \(\mathrm{CsSn}_{2}\mathrm{Br}_{5}\)), (2) alkali metal halides, (LiF, LiI, KCl, NaBr, or CsF), or (3) some group 14 oxides \(\mathrm{GeO}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) are the best candidates to protect the Sn perovskite surface.[171] Here, the authors Wei et al. suggest similar materials may benefit Pb-Sn mixed PSCs as well and should be explored further, considering the lattice mismatch and charge transport properties. For Pb-Sn PSCs, low-dimensional perovskites are already used and will be further discussed in Section 3.4.4, and although the metal halide treatments are not widely reported, other salt systems have gained increasing attention in this field. From the recently reported surface treatments, much of the high-performing Pb-Sn perovskites were achieved using guanidinium \((\mathrm{GA}^{+})\) and/or thiocyanate \((\mathrm{SCN}^{-})\) containing salts such as GASCN,[39] GABr,[7,44] KSCN,[142] and Py-SCN.[41]
The first GASCN treatment was employed by Tong et al, in 2019 which proved to give high efficiency of \(20.5\%\) from a \((\mathrm{FASnI}_{3})_{0.6}\) \((\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3})_{0.4}\) perovskite absorber, with remarkable charge carrier lifetimes of \(>1\upmu\mathrm{s}\), charge carrier diffusion lengths of \(\sim2.5~\upmu\mathrm{m}\) and an order of magnitude reduction in defect densities. (Figure 15a).[39] Here, GASCN was reported to passivate the grain boundaries which reduced the defect states in surface regions, by forming 2D structures (with yet unknown compositions) which segregate at the grain boundaries. The much wider bandgap of 2D structures passivate the boundaries improving the electronics properties and block Sn diffusion out of the grains and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) diffusion into the grains suppressing the Sn oxidation.[39] In another study employing \(\mathrm{GA}^{+}\) salts, GABr was used to passivate the \(\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{FA}_{0.79}\mathrm{MA}_{0.16}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) triple cation perovskite film by Jayawardena et al. achieving the highest reported fill factor \((\sim83\%)\) for Pb-Sn mixed perovskite absorbers to date with an efficiency of \(14.4\%\) (Figure 15b).[7] In Pb-Sn mixed perovskites, fill factors of \(>80\%\) are seldom reported, and the authors attributed the improvements to surface passivation by salts of large \(\mathrm{GA}^{+}\) cation aggregated on the surface, as well as the electronic band structure improvements arising from the small \(\mathrm{Br}^{-}\)ions diffusing through the thickness of the perovskite film, producing a more electron doped perovskite layer.[7] Recently, in another study, \(12\%\) of GABr was introduced to a \(\mathrm{FA}_{0.7}\mathrm{MA}_{0.3}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.3}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) by Zhou et al., with an efficiency of \(20.63\%\) \((19.8\%\) certified).[44] Here, high charge carrier concentrations with reduced trap densities and a reduction of \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}\) deficit were reported due to defect passivation by GABr, with improved thermal \((90\%\) retention of initial PCE) and environmental stability \((85\%\) retention of initial stability) in air without encapsulation (Figure 15c). Generally, all studies on \(\mathrm{GA}^{+}\) salt treatments reported higher grain sizes and defect passivation. Since Pb-Sn perovskites face arising of a large number of defects due to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation, GA treatments offer great promise and potential in achieving higher efficiencies and stabilities in these PSCs.
As mentioned above, in the GASCN treatment, formation of a 2D structure was reported.[39] In another study employing \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\), Li et al.[41] reported a \(1.5~\mathrm{mol\%}\) incorporation of pyrrolidinium thiocyanate (PySCN) to form a 1D perovskite \(\mathrm{PySn}_{x}\mathrm{Pb}_{1-x}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) on the surface of \(\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{FA}_{0.85}\mathrm{MA}_{0.1}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) to passivate the grain boundaries and the film surface which resulted in \(1.1~\upmu\mathrm{m}\) charge carrier lifetimes and \(20.4\%\) PCE.[41] The perovskite was fabricated through the two-step BIG process (Section 3.3.1), and PySCN was directly incorporated in the precursor solution resulting in the surface 1D perovskite upon fabrication of the absorber film. With this treatment, the improved HTL/perovskite junction qualities led to enhancements of \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}\), FF and the high charge carrier lifetimes reported.[41] In other work, Chen et al. used KSCN in a \(\mathrm{MA}_{0.5}\mathrm{FA}_{0.5}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) perovskite.[42] (Figure 16) resulting in high-quality perovskite films with no pinholes, enhanced crystallinity, and enlarged grain sizes which improved the device PCE owing to a smooth carrier transportation in the film. Here, KSCN was incorporated directly in the precursor film (range of \(1-10\%\)), and \(7\%\) was found to aid the best performance. In agreement with the previous researchers, the authors suggest that the \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)ions interact better than \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)ions with \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) in the perovskite system, which allows them to protect \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) from oxidizing as well as allowing slow crystallization of the films. It was also noted that the synergistic effect of both \(\mathrm{K}^{+}\)and \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)added together works better in this instance, than adding \(\mathrm{K}^{+}\)or \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)alone in the form of other salts, as evident in Figure 16c where \(7\%\) KSCN results in a compact film with large grains. During the annealing process, the \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)was almost completely removed as HSCN
gas from the film, whereas the \(\mathsf{K}^{+}\)ions may have segregated at the grain boundaries passivating the film.[142]
In most high-performing Sn-only perovskites, incorporating \(\mathrm{EDAI}_{2}\) (ethylenediammonium diiodide) is widely reported to aid passivating the defect states on the surface, reducing pinholes improving film morphology, and alleviating \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation.[46,158,172] Interestingly, many of these devices were found to improve their PCE over time, reaching a maximum after being stored in inert atmosphere for over two months ("slow passivation"). This approach could be further explored in Pb-Sn mixed perovskites as another possible passivation pathway.
## 3.4.4. Use of Low-Dimensional Perovskites
As mentioned in the previous section, some \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)passivation layers can give rise to low-dimensional (1D or 2D) perovskites. Similar perovskites, such as 2D Ruddlesden–Popper or 2D Dion–Jacobson structures (Figure 17a and b, respectively), can be used either as functional passivation layers for bulk 3D perovskites or as active layers themselves and is worth exploring further. Interfacial engineering with a 2D perovskite passivation layer first gained significant attention in 2017 when the option of a 2D/3D interface lead to devices that were stable for over \(10\ 000\ \mathrm{h}\).[173] Due to the reduced dimensionality of 2D perovskites, their bandgaps are larger than 3D perovskite counterparts, and the reduced dimensionality and use of large A-site cations make them ideal candidates for passivation, which can decrease unwanted \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) or ion migration within devices. Zeng et al. reported a decrease in nonradiative recombination in a 3D Pb-Sn mixed perovskite when a 2D passivation layer was used, increasing PCE from \(12.5\%\) to \(15\%\) .[174] Li et al. showed that the phenethylammonium \((\mathrm{PEA}^{+})\) A-site can also be used to passivate \(\Gamma^{-}\)and \(\mathrm{FA}^{+}\)trap states on the surface of a Pb-Sn perovskite active layer.[175]
Reducing the dimensionality of the perovskite structure from 3D toward a 2D structure presents an opportunity for mixed Pb-Sn materials, as they have been previously shown to regulate the crystallization process, suppress the oxidation of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\), and reduce the environmental impact relative to pure-Pb perovskites.[176–179] The reduced dimensionality of \((\mathrm{PEA})_{2}(\mathrm{FA})_{n-1}\mathrm{Sn}_{n}\mathrm{I}_{3n+1}\) also showed improved thermodynamic stability relative to \(\mathrm{FAsnI}_{3}\) via a reduced decomposition enthalpy with respect to a common oxidation pathway. This was also shown to improve rapidly as n is reduced. This effect, when combined with the hydrophobic nature of the PEA molecule, allows for high device stabilities in Sn-containing 2D and quasi-2D \((1<n<6)\) perovskites.[177] Pb-Sn materials also have the distinct advantage of reducing the bandgap relative to pure-Pb perovskites, especially in quasi-2D perovskites of the form \(\mathrm{A1}_{2}\mathrm{A}_{2n-1}\mathrm{B}_{n}\mathrm{X}_{3n+1}\) or \(\mathrm{A1A2_{n-1}B_{n}X_{3n+1}}\), where \(\mathfrak{n}>1\), as this reduces the bandgap relative to pure 2D phase perovskites, while still maintaining higher stability than 3D perovskites. A bandgap of \(1.27\ \mathrm{eV}\) has recently been shown for (3AMP) \((\mathrm{MA}_{0.5}\mathrm{FA}_{0.5})_{3}(\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sn}_{0.5})_{4}\mathrm{I}_{3}\), which was used to improve the device properties of a 3D mixed Pb-Sn perovskite, with improved power conversion efficiency of \(20.09\%\) for the mixed Pb-Sn 2D/3D configuration.[43] Much like 3D perovskites, 2D mixed Pb-Sn perovskites show a bandgap bowing as a function of increasing Sn content. Due to the reduced dimensionality of 2D perovskites, the band edge dispersion is reduced, resulting in a lower bandgap bowing than 3D perovskites. The bandgap bowing of 2D structures is also less pronounced than that of the 3D structures, with values ranging from 0.352 to \(0.918\ \mathrm{eV}\) (Figure 17c) relative to \(1.08\ \mathrm{eV}\) shown for \(\mathrm{MAPb}_{1-x}\mathrm{Sn}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{3}\).
## 4. Outlook and Future Directions
## 4.1. Review Summary and Highlights
In summary, Pb-Sn mixed perovskites have gained increasing attention especially due to their suitability as narrow-bandgap subcells in all-perovskite tandem solar cells. The Pb-Sn metal cation composition in the B site of \(\mathrm{ABX}_{3}\) perovskite structure directly contributes in tuning the "narrow" bandgap of the absorber generally in the range of \(1.2\ -\) \(1.3\ \mathrm{eV}\), while the X (halide) ion aids in fine-tuning the bandgap further. With the B site, the bandgap shows a "bowing effect" where it decreases with increasing Sn content up to about \(30–60\%\) of the B site and then increases when Sn is further increased. In this range, these perovskites can achieve bandgap energies closer to the Shockley–Queisser limit of \(\sim1.3\ \mathrm{eV}\). While reaching this optimum bandgap, various efforts have been taken to increase the efficiencies of these PSCs, achieving \(21.7\%\) (20.7% certified) in 2020, the highest reported by the time of this review. However, the maximum performance achievable with these devices has not yet been reached, largely due to the prominent issue of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation. As such, the efforts taken toward efficiency improvements of Pb-Sn mixed PSCs highlighted in this review can be summarized under three main categories as discussed below:
i) \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation issue and surface passivation: A number of studies tackling the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) issue can be found, the most common being \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\) incorporation into the precursor solution. Additionally, \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\cdot3\mathrm{FACl}\), \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\cdot\) -pyrazine complexes, ascorbic acid, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2}\) acid have also been reported. Additives such as these and solvent annealing (e.g., using DMF vapor) also aid in achieving better quality grains and large grain sizes up to micron sizes, in addition to inhibiting \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation. However, very high efficiencies were recently achieved by incorporating a small amount of Sn metallic powder in the precursor, as well as zwitterion incorporation to suppress the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) formation in the Pb-Sn perovskite absorbers. Other factors such as the amount of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) incorporated in the cells (\(>\!50\%\) Pb preferred), choice of anti-solvent (toluene preferred), and surface treatments using agents such as \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)salts can also suppress the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) formation.
According to recent studies, the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation is more dominant on the perovskite surface and not in the bulk, and the surface \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) acts as deep electron traps.[180] Due to this large number of defects inherent in the Sn-containing perovskite films, some form of surface passivation is highly recommended. Organic salts containing \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)and \(\mathrm{GA}^{+}\) (such as GASCN, GABr, KSCN, and MASCN) have been recently gaining increasing attention as modes of surface and grain boundary passivation of Pb-Sn mixed perovskites. Various improvements in PSCs leading to efficiency improvements have been reported, including larger grains, grain boundary passivation by formation of 1D or 2D structures, altering the perovskite composition such as Br doping giving rise to better electronic band alignment of the perovskite with interlayers have been reported, along with suppressed \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation due to limited diffusion of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into the perovskite lattice. Reducing dimensionality of the Pb-Sn mixed perovskites has also proved to mitigate \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation, through promoting higher thermodynamic stability of the resulting perovskite. Other structures such as alkali metal halides, various oxides such as \(\mathrm{GeO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) are also potential pathways for future research, as suggested by theoretical studies on Sn-only perovskites, as well as other organic cations such as EDA.
ii) Crystallization and film quality: It is commonly accepted that high-quality perovskite films (compact, pinhole-free, highly crystalline with
large grain sizes) directly contribute to high-performing PSCs. Pb-Sn mixed perovskites are known to achieve better surface coverage compared to Pb-only analogues owing to the "flower-like" patterns of grain
growth.[1,12] The choice of a proper solvent system such as DMSO incorporated DMF has enabled better control over the grain growth, through DMSO-\(\mathrm{SnI}_{2}\) adduct formation, while employing specific fabrication
techniques such as vapor deposition have enabled \(>5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) grain sizes. \(\mathrm{Br}^{-}\) or \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) incorporation in Pb-Sn perovskites has also given rise to large grain sizes, high crystallinity, functionalization of grain boundaries, and reduction of electronic disorder, resulting in higher charge carrier lifetimes, carrier mobilities leading to improved PSC performances.
techniques such as vapor deposition have enabled \(>5~\upmu\mathrm{m}\) grain sizes. \(\mathrm{Br}^{-}\) or \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) incorporation in Pb-Sn perovskites has also given rise to large grain sizes, high crystallinity, functionalization of grain boundaries, and reduction of electronic disorder, resulting in higher charge carrier lifetimes, carrier mobilities leading to improved PSC performances.Moreover, different pre- and post-treatments during the fabrication of the perovskite film have been employed such as anti-solvent treatment and various vacuum and solvent annealing techniques. Vacuum-assisted crystallization methods allow large and controlled grain growth and generally preferred over anti-solvent treatments that need high precision and purity. However, possibly due to the ease of use, anti-solvent method is still seen to be the primary crystallization technique used in Pb-Sn mixed perovskites, generally followed by a thermal annealing step (commonly \(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(20\ \mathrm{min}\)). Multi-step deposition or multi-step annealing is also seen to produce better results than one-step processes and need to be explored further.
The perovskite composition (including the Pb:Sn ratio) and the film thickness are other factors that strongly affect the grain sizes and morphology.[15] Grain size increments from \(\sim300\) to \(\sim1000\ \mathrm{nm}\) were reported upon increasing the film thicknesses from \(400\ \mathrm{nm}\) to \(1010\ \mathrm{nm}\), also enabling longer charge carrier lifetimes.[15] Most reports achieving high efficiencies generally report reductions in trap density and recombination in the resulting films.
(iii) Selection of interlayers: The importance of suitable interlayers should also be noted here, for a more successful suppression of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation in the PSCs. PEDOT:PSS is still widely used as HTL for the Pb-Sn mixed perovskites, along with ITO transparent electrode to compose the anode. Previous work has indicated that the quality and morphology of Pb-Sn perovskite films are dependent on the nature of the charge transport layer on which they are fabricated. Favorable effects of an organic/polymer HTL over a metal oxide: ETL on the resulting Pb-Sn perovskite film is one of the reasons for the prevailing preference for
inverted architectures. Other HTLs such as PTAA and \(\mathrm{NiO_{x}}\) have been explored with improvements compared to PEDOT:PSS. Although PEDOT is notorious for its acidity inducing corrosion of ITO electrode, and the fact it can accelerate the \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation with residual moisture, a shift to a different HTL is not yet seen at large in the Pb-Sn mixed PSC community, thus should be further explored. To compose the cathode in these inverted devices, \(\mathrm{C}_{60}/\mathrm{BCP/Ag}\) or PCBM/BCP/Ag are most frequently used as ETL/metal electrode combinations, though other materials such as \(_{\mathrm{ZnO}}\) or LiF are reported in a limited number of studies.
## 4.2. Current Applications: Tandem Solar Cells
As discussed earlier, the performance of single-junction PSCs is limited by the Shockley–Queisser limit, which usually falls at around \(33\%^{[109]}\) and tandem cell structures are being explored as a solution. All-perovskite tandem cell structures can be fabricated using printing technologies that can result in high-performing devices with low-cost manufacturability. The bandgap of Pb-based PSCs usually falls in the range of \(1.48\,-\,2.3\,\mathrm{\eV},^{[3]}\) which makes them excellent candidates for the wide-bandgap/top cell with Pb-Sn mixed PSCs \((\ 1.2\ -\ 1.3\ \mathrm{eV})^{[181]}\) for the narrowbandgap/bottom cell of an all-perovskite tandem structure. Tandem cell structures usually consist of two or more PSCs connected together in either a 2-terminal (2T) configuration or a 4-terminal (4T) configuration.[6] In a 2T or monolithic tandem configuration, the top and bottom devices are fabricated with interconnecting layers (ICLs) between them whereas in a 4T configuration the
top and bottom cells are mechanically stacked on top of each other and connected in series using electrodes. The main benefit of 2T tandem solar cells is their simplicity and the low cost of fabrication due to the possibility of using low-temperature processing techniques such as solution processing. However, in this case, the challenge is in precise current matching between the top and bottom cells to optimize the cell performance as well as fabricating ICLs that can act as a barrier which prevents the dissolution of the bottom cell when depositing the respective top cell, along with minimal optical and voltage losses.[6] Even though the 4T tandem architecture does not require strict bandgap matching between top and bottom cells, it requires two extra electrodes to build a connection between the two cells which increases the manufacturing cost of tandem cells with such architectures.
The potential of an all-perovskite tandem cell was first published in late 2014 via optical modeling where the researchers presented a simple monolithic configuration where the top and the bottom layers of the tandem were separated by the HTL and the ETL between them.[182] Before incorporating Pb-Sn mixed perovskites, the first experimentally reported all-perovskite tandem solar cell comprised of a 4T structure consisting of a \(\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}\) \((\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{g}}{=}~2.3~\mathrm{eV})\) top cell and \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) \((\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{g}}{=}\) \(1.55~\mathrm{eV})\) bottom cell. The device was reported to achieve a \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}\) of \(2.24\mathrm{~V~}\) in series connection and a PCE of \(8.30\%\) .[183] Heo et al. in 2015 demonstrated a tandem cell with a 2T configuration which was fabricated by sandwiching the two substrates containing the top \(\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3}\) \((\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{g}}{=}\ 2.25\ \mathrm{eV})\) cell and bottom \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) \((\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{g}}{=}\ 1.55\ \mathrm{eV})\) cell together by pressuring with a double clip and subsequent drying. This
technique yielded the first publication of a monolithic tandem solar cell which had a PCE of \(10.8\%\) and a \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}\) of 1.95 V.[184]
Use of \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed perovskites in a tandem structure was first published by Yang et al. where wide-bandgap perovskite with a \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) absorption layer was used as the top cell and a narrow-bandgap perovskite with a \(\mathrm{MA}_{0.5}\mathrm{FA}_{0.5}\mathrm{Pb}_{0.5}\mathrm{Sr}_{0.25}\mathrm{I}_{3}\) \((\mathrm{E_{g}}=\mathrm{E_{g}}=1.38\ \mathrm{eV})\) was used to fabricate a 4T tandem solar cell that achieved a combined PCE of \(19.08\%\) .[111] On 2T architectures, the first reported perovskite/perovskite tandem cell was made using a \(1.2\ \mathrm{eV}\) bandgap bottom cell with perovskite composition \(\mathrm{FA_{0.75}C s_{0.25}S n_{0.5}P b_{0.5}I_{3}}\) and a top cell with a bandgap of \(1.8~\mathrm{eV}\) and composition \(\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.17}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{0.5}\mathrm{Br}_{0.5})_{3}\) .[11] The monolithic cell was able to achieve an efficiency of \(16.9\%\) and a \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}\) of \(1.66\mathrm{~V~}\) . As of present, the highest reported monolithic all-perovskite solar cell has achieved a PCE of \(24.8\%\) and a \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}\) of \(1.96~\mathrm{V}\) while retaining \(90\%\) of its initial performance after operating for \(436~\mathrm{h}\) under 1- sun illumination at maximum power point.[34] This shows promising stability for the \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed perovskite, which was fabricated using metallic Sn powder to implement stability as discussed in Section 3.4.2. The tandem device consisted of a top cell with an absorption layer \(\mathrm{Cs_{0.2}F A_{0.8}P b I_{1.8}B r_{1.2}}\) \((\mathrm{E_{g}}=1.77~\mathrm{eV})\) and a bottom cell with an absorption layer \(\mathrm{FA_{0.7}M A_{0.3}P b_{0.5}S n_{0.5}I_{3}}\) \((\mathrm{E_{g}}~=~1.22~\mathrm{eV})\) .[34] With further
optimization of \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed PSC bottom cells along with the ICLs, all-perovskite tandem solar cells show a promising future to overcome the performance limitations of single-junction devices through simple fabrication techniques.
## 4.3. Future Directions
In improving \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) PSCs, it should be noted here that the major efficiency improvements are still seen when the surface passivation or \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) suppression techniques were employed. The ultimate goal in the development of \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) PSCs, as with any PV device, is their largescale manufacturing. When fabricating tandem solar cells, unlike \(\mathrm{Pb}\) -only wide-bandgap PSCs, the ambient air manufacturing of \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) perovskites is still a challenge due to the ambient instability discussed. With increasing Sn content, the crystallization of the perovskite becomes faster[185] and is more difficult to control in a roll-to-roll process. With various strides made in increasing the device stability and efficiency, \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) PSC research must now explore more large area fabrication techniques, and ambient fabrication techniques, to point the field toward real-world applications. Only a limited number of reports are currently available for \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) PSCs large area fabrication, such as the study by Li et al, which discusses \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed PSCs with \(1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\) active area using doctor blading technique.[130] Along with the techniques to suppress \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) oxidation, proper encapsulation methods need to be secured to achieve longterm stability of the cells against moisture and
oxygen.[186] Another challenge in high-throughput manufacturing of \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) PSCs is the toxicity of the solvents used such as DMF and DMSO.[6] Compared to the \(\mathrm{Pb}\) -only perovskites, alternative green solvents have not been widely studied for \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed PSCs and should be investigated further. More importantly, with the recent findings in \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) -oxo- only perovskites where DMSO was found to contribute to the 137] testing alternate solvent systems is an important path for research in \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) perovskites. Thus, as future directions, the passivation of grain boundaries and reducing surface and bulk trap densities are key points in enhancing the performance of \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed perovskite films. As discussed so far, much of the field of \(\mathrm{Pb-Sn}\) mixed perovskites is still to be explored, and there is a large number of interdependent factors affecting the device performance. Thus, a collective effort from the researchers to understand and improve the most crucial parameters is critical in reaching the maximum benefit of these devices.
